data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","sequence_number":1748,"item_id":"msg_0cda719d9ce44a33016b07a426eff887d186260dbd57ac74b2","output_index":1,"content_index":0,"text":"{\"queries\":[],\"result_json\":\"{\\\"reviews\\\":[{\\\"actor\\\":\\\"wren\\\",\\\"decision\\\":\\\"develop\\\",\\\"ability\\\":null,\\\"reason\\\":\\\"The supplied related-capability search was exhausted. hand-twist-fiber-cord covers cord manufacture, inspection and straight opposing-hand trials, but not forming a knot or testing its changed geometry; probe does not supply that missing repeatable mechanism. Develop a general bare-hand cord-knot operation, reusing the existing cord representation and trial code rather than claiming their reinvention. The reported straight-cord result at tick 232 is not evidence of knot feasibility or knotted strength. Missing bend/contact mechanics belong in the current development handoff, not a permanent rejection.\\\",\\\"specification\\\":\\\"General operation: hand-tie-cord-knot(targetId, mode, knot, workId?, trialForce?, trialDuration?). Modes: attempt, inspect, trial, untie, cancel; initially supported knot: overhand in a single cord. Any applicable actor may learn it. No tools, anchors, attachments, extra fiber, other participant or stock are supplied. Exact initial target reference is item-226-wren-2532, but the interface accepts any applicable owned cord. Re-read the complete authoritative item at execution: its supplied record is paged and incomplete. Do not reconstruct omitted fields from the fragment or overwrite current condition with the older trial value.\\\\nPrerequisites: a completed, accessible, owned, unreserved cord with represented length, diameter, material, condition and strand structure; two available usable hands, adequate energy, dry stable footing and the existing cord-trial personal-clearance checks. No prior knot success or cord-making mastery is presumed. Reject invalid ownership, missing target or unsupported arguments without charge. Unsupported material mechanics must return a specific limitation, not a fabricated response.\\\\nCosts and progress: study is 3 individually paid rounds at 0.2 energy per round. Attempt reserves one target, then performs three paid rounds at 0.5 energy each: lay out and gently bend a loop, pass the end through, then dress and release-check. Every round records the actual operation, endpoint positions, material length allocated to tails and knot path, condition changes and stage reached. A measured geometric or bending failure terminates at that stage, charges only work actually performed and preserves the resulting cord state. Inspection costs 1 round and 0.2 energy and reports accessible visible geometry and recorded tests; it supplies no load trial. Untie is at most two paid rounds of 0.5 energy each, reversing accessible crossings and releasing the cord; a jammed or damaged knot may resist, and a failed attempt leaves the actual configuration. Trial costs 1 round and 0.5 energy, with requested force greater than zero and at most 10 N, and requested duration greater than zero and at most 60 seconds, also bounded by the simulation round duration. No implicit trial follows tying. Cancel costs no energy and stops future work.\\\\nMechanism and implementation dependency: create a versioned cord-knot law in this conversation's development handoff. It must represent an overhand crossing sequence and actual centerline geometry, conserve centerline length, check nonpenetration, finite cord thickness, bending limits, accessible tails, and hand grip spans at each step. Minimum bend radius, safe hand manipulation bounds, contact/friction, tightening/sliding, bending damage and knot stress concentration must be explicit code parameters supported by the material implementation, not inferred from the previous straight pull or invented as observations. Reuse the reed material/strand law and cord volume bookkeeping from hand-twist-fiber-cord and record their exact implementation references and reused helpers. Add only the missing knot mechanics. Do not silently assume knot efficiency, friction, flexibility or required tail length. The developer must supply those executable rules before enabling the affected physical transition; unresolved portions report unavailable rather than succeeding. The smallest feasible precursor is the paid layout/bend attempt and inspection, recording measured infeasibility or actual partial geometry; successful end passage, dressing and load trial remain gated on the corresponding implemented mechanics. No fixed-era unlock or unrelated activity sequence is required.\\\\nTrial integration: reuse hand-twist-fiber-cord's opposing-hand force, grip, extension, creep, strand damage and fragment-conservation machinery, explicitly recording that reuse. Extend its geometry and loading path to the knotted cord instead of applying a straight-span test unchanged. Both tails must permit independent opposing grips without gripping the knot; derive available spans from current geometry. Reject an impossible grip without applying force. Use the existing actor and grip limits in addition to the 10 N request cap. Account for local bending/contact stress and sliding with the new law. Stop on grip loss, knot slip, strand failure, loss of required tail geometry or actor interruption, and report the attained force history and seconds actually held, not the requested values. Unknown knot load mechanics must prevent a claimed trial result. A held result establishes only that configuration at that achieved force for that actual duration, never a safe working rating or improved strength.\\\\nConservation and lifecycle: tying changes the same item's configuration; it does not create a second cord, length, quantity or attachments. Preserve origin, ownership, processing history and material volume. Represent any fragments, detached fibers or scrap as partitions of the original material with provenance; do not duplicate quantity when creating child records. Elastic extension and retained deformation follow the reused material law. A knot can shorten usable span but does not consume its allocated cord length. Apply ordinary ageing and existing wear exactly once; add only damage caused by actual modeled manipulations or trials. Paid stage progress persists. Insufficient energy, a missed work round, lost reach or invalid footing pauses before the next charge. Resume by workId after rechecking target, geometry and prerequisites; never repay completed stages. Cancellation releases reservations once and leaves partial loops, tight knots, damage and products as they actually are; it does not automatically untie or refund energy, elapsed study or work. Death releases reservations once and leaves the physical cord and any fragments at the actor's location under existing ownership/death rules. Inspection and maintenance do not run autonomously. A resting knot persists as geometry and experiences only implemented ordinary ageing/environmental effects; no continuing force, happiness or invisible maintenance is supplied. Any future wear, loosening under load or repair requires the corresponding actual process. Development and review waiting advance no world time.\\\\nEvidence: emit versioned events for each paid physical stage, infeasible geometry, interruption, untying, achieved trial interval, slip and damage. Reference exact actor, target and work IDs, implementation versions, tick, before/after geometry and condition, requested versus achieved force/time, and resulting fragment IDs. Store bounded personal findings linked to those events, including informative failures. Established validation, conservation, deduplication and lifecycle rules execute in code rather than returning each attempt for model review. Do not disclose hidden material properties as observed facts.\\\",\\\"learning\\\":3,\\\"experienceReview\\\":\\\"No experience benefit from requesting, review, registration, naming, study completion or mastery claims. No automatic happiness, reward or comfort from a knot. Initially grant no additional pleasure or satisfaction effect: this request requires physical attempts and evidence only. Record verified information gain, including informative failure, separately from mood; repeated inspection or the same unchanged outcome is not new information. If a later existing experience rule recognizes the actual event for this actor, use its functional deduplication, bounded cap, cooldown and decay rather than adding a knot-specific reward channel. Leave protected reward accounts, budgets, goals and permissions unchanged, and do not reduce basic nutrition by mood or bypass the physical-comfort cap of 10.\\\",\\\"visualReview\\\":\\\"Provide reusable cord-knot visual metadata keyed to actual item and work state: material/color from the cord, diameter, centerline/control points, crossings, loop dimensions, tail lengths, knot location, stage, tightening state, condition, fraying and fragment IDs. Emit hand manipulation and pull visuals only during performed actions; show slack, incomplete crossings, slipping, broken strands or infeasibility as such. A partial loop must not display a completed-knot icon. A completed overhand appearance requires the recorded crossing and release-check outcome, and a held-trial indicator requires an achieved-force/duration event. Distinguish appearance, formation success and tested behavior; none implies strength certification. Reuse existing cord and pull-trial visual components and record that reuse, adding only knot-path and knot-state metadata. No success animation or reward overlay for refused, paused, unavailable or failed actions.\\\"}]}\"}","logprobs":[]}

